{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>It is joy to the righteous to do judgment, but dismay to the workers of iniquity (Proverbs 21:15).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 21) that judgment, through which the world is sustained, is dismay and terror to the wicked and joy to the righteous. Know that the word שמחה (joy) is greater than גילה (exultation), רינה (singing), and משוש (gladness); as it is written (Proverbs 23:24): ״גיל יגיל אבי צדיק ויולד חכם ישמח בו״ — the father of the righteous will greatly exult, and he who begets a wise son will rejoice in him — he used the term גילה for the righteous, and the term שמחה, which is greater, for the wise man who possesses both qualities, wise and righteous; for Solomon, peace be upon him, does not praise a wicked wise man. And so it is written (Psalms 32:11): ״שמחו בה' וגילו צדיקים והרנינו כל ישרי לב״ — rejoice in HaShem and exult, O righteous; and shout for joy, all you upright of heart — he joined together in this verse three terms of joy: שמחה (rejoicing), גילה (exultation), and רינה (shouting for joy), and he mentioned שמחה first, for it is the greatest, and attributed it to the Name, may He be exalted, because of its surpassing merit; for he did not say 'rejoice and exult in HaShem, O righteous' (שמחו וגילו בה' צדיקים), but rather 'rejoice in HaShem' first. And the meaning of the verse is that the righteous man rejoices when judgment is executed in the city and there is no robbery and violence among them; and if oppression of the poor and robbery of judgment and justice are seen in the province, he will worry and sigh over this, for such is the way of the righteous. And 'it is dismay to the workers of iniquity' — the execution of judgment is dismay and fear and terror to the workers of iniquity, for they fear for their souls when evildoers are called to account in the city, and the matter is for them a dismay, lest they be punished for their evil deeds. If so, the verse has explained to you that the doing of judgment, which is dismay to the workers of iniquity, is joy to the righteous." "<b>And behold,</b> Solomon, peace be upon him, intended by this to inform us that a person is obligated to be joyful in the commandments when he performs them or sees others performing them; and this is why he said 'to do judgment' (עשות משפט) and did not say 'to do it judgment' (עשותו משפט). Know that rejoicing in the performance of the commandments is itself a commandment in its own right, and just as the commandment is service to the Name, may He be blessed, so too the joy over the commandment is called service; thus it is written (Deuteronomy 28:47): ״תחת אשר לא עבדת את ה' אלהיך בשמחה״ — because you did not serve HaShem your Elohim with joy; and it is written (Psalms 100:2): ״עבדו את ה' בשמחה״ — serve HaShem with joy — explaining that joy is the perfection of service. Therefore the matter of song in the Temple and the Tabernacle, both the song of the mouth and the instrument, is what brings a person's soul to the path of joy; and from here Scripture says of the Levites 'to perform the service of the service' (לעבוד עבודת עבודה), and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: what is the service of the service? This is the song, for the Levites were commanded and obligated to sing and to awaken joy over the commandment of the sacrifice, so that the performance of the commandment would be with joy. And it is well known that the prime time and vigor of a person — for the fitness of the power of the voice and for the exertion of labors — is from the age of thirty years to fifty, as they said: 'at thirty, for strength' (בן שלשים לכח), and from fifty onward the strength diminishes and the voice fails and the Levite is disqualified; for how shall he sing the song of HaShem when his voice has failed and changed? And this is what we learned: Levites with blemishes are fit, but with respect to age they are disqualified — and concerning this Scripture says (Numbers 8:25): ״ומבן חמשים שנה ישוב מצבא העבודה ולא יעבוד עוד״ — and from fifty years of age he shall return from the army of the service and shall serve no more; and because of this the Levites were commanded to be counted from thirty years old up to fifty, which is the time of their service. And this is what is written." "<b>Take the sum of the sons of Gershon, them also, by their fathers' houses, by their families, from thirty years old and upward even unto fifty years old.</b> It says 'them also' (גם הם) — that is, just as you counted the sons of Kohath; for so it mentioned in the section above. And since the sons of Kohath were first in rank, for they carried the sacred vessels, therefore He commanded to count them first, even though Gershon was the firstborn. And I have already mentioned in the preceding section that just as Israel in the wilderness had four standards for the four directions of the world, so the Levites had four divisions for the four directions of the world: Moses and Aaron and his sons to the east, the sons of Gershon to the west, the sons of Kohath to the south, and the sons of Merari to the north."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:31.723320940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}